  "description": "Whether you're making Texas-style chile con carne, a quick ground-beef-and-canned-bean chili, or even a vegan or vegetarian version, the best thing you can do to up your chili game is to start with real, honest-to-goodness whole dried chilies.",
  "notes": "Notes: Chile purée can be substituted about 4:1 for chili powder (i.e., use four tablespoons of purée for every tablespoon of powder called for in a recipe). It can be stored by freezing it in an ice cube tray, popping out the cubes, and placing them in a freezer bag for up to six months.",
